[38;2;124;124;124mdifferential is the (local) limit of $f'_n$ in $L^1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mDefinition: $f \in L^1_{loc}(\Omega)$ is weakly differentiable if there exists [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$g \in L^1_{loc}(\Omega)$ such that for all test functions $\varphi \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mC_c^\infty(\Omega)$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\int_\Omega f \varphi' = -\int_\Omega g \varphi$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe statement claims equivalence between weak differentiability and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexistence of smooth approximations with Cauchy derivatives.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Forward direction (weakly differentiable ⟹ such sequence exists):**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $f$ is weakly differentiable with weak derivative $g$, both in $L^1_{loc}$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthen by mollification $f_\epsilon = \rho_\epsilon * f$ is smooth, $f_\epsilon [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\to f$ in $L^1_{loc}$, and $f'_\epsilon = \rho_\epsilon * g \to g$ in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^1_{loc}$. Since $f'_\epsilon \to g$ in $L^1_{loc}$, it's Cauchy in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^1_{loc}$. So forward direction holds.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Reverse direction (such sequence exists ⟹ weakly differentiable):**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSuppose $f_n$ smooth, $f_n \to f$ in $L^1_{loc}$, and $f'_n$ is Cauchy in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^1_{loc}$. Then $f'_n \to g$ in $L^1_{loc}$ for some $g \in L^1_{loc}$ (since [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$L^1_{loc}$ is complete on compact subsets... actually $L^1_{loc}$ completeness:[0m[38;2;124;124;124mCauchy in $L^1_{loc}$ means Cauchy in $L^1$ on every compact, so converges in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^1$ on every compact to some $g$, and $g \in L^1_{loc}$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow for any test function $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ with support in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact $K$:[0m
